<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 406–453</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Attila the Hun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attila was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> from 434, at first jointly with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a>, and sole ruler from about 445 until his death in 453. From the Hungarian plain he dominated many Germanic and steppe peoples, extracted heavy tribute from the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 451, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> checked him at the Catalaunian Plains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 452; his empire broke up soon after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attila.jpg" alt="Attila" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julio Strozza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attila_Hunnorum_Rex_Flagellum_Dei_Aquilejae_Eversor_Utini_Instaurator.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Others served Rome: Alans under Sangiban fought on the Roman side against <b>Attila</b> at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Frankish federates fought the Vandals as they approached the Rhine in 406, and Frankish troops served under Aetius against <b>Attila</b> at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, while other Franks backed the Huns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huns received great quantities of gold as mercenaries and as tribute, and Denis Sinor argued that under <b>Attila</b> they depended almost entirely on plunder and Roman payments; Roman gold coins circulated throughout their empire, though the European Huns struck none of their own.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 451 Theodoric fought beside Flavius Aetius against <b>Attila</b> at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains and was killed as the Huns were driven back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Rouran, who made khagan the title of their supreme ruler, held the eastern steppe in turn, while in the west the Huns built an empire that under <b>Attila</b>, sole ruler from about 445 to 453, drew a tribute of 2,100 pounds of gold a year from Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching west into Belgica with Gepids, Ostrogoths, Alans and other vassals, <b>Attila</b> captured Metz, killing most of its people and burning the town; he also took Strasbourg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=436" aria-label="Pannonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5003 578 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Attila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5453 848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5453 848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=436">What was here in 436 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No spear shall harm those who are sure to live; and those who are sure to die Fate overtakes even in peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech before the battle of the Catalaunian Plains, 451 (Jordanes, Getica, 39) &#183; tr. Charles C. Mierow, 1915 &#183; Jordanes' account, written a century later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14809/pg14809-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For what is war but your usual custom? Or what is sweeter for a brave man than to seek revenge with his own hand?&#8221;</p><footer>Speech before the battle of the Catalaunian Plains, 451 (Jordanes, Getica, 39) &#183; tr. Charles C. Mierow, 1915 &#183; Jordanes' account, written a century later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14809/pg14809-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
